% The smallest model in which int_ne is the only thing that can make progress.
%
% ne_sat.fzn is the model this one exists to correct. It also carries an int_ne, but it
% carries int_lt(x, y) beside it, and int_lt alone fixes x = 1, y = 2 -- so ne_sat
% verifies whether or not the disequality does anything at all. test_prop.ml's header
% says so in as many words, and this project has now been bitten five times by an
% instance that could not see the thing it was testing break (D-0009, D-0010, D-0012,
% D-0017, D-0018).
%
% Here nothing but the disequality is posted. Root propagation infers nothing (with two
% unfixed terms a disequality has nothing to say), so the search must decide x = 1, and
% then int_ne must remove 1 from y for y = 2 to be found. That removal is also a *bound*
% move, so it is the path I-P5 and M1-T17's bug B are about: before M1-T17 it emitted
% `rup +1 y_ge_2 >= 1 ;`, a line asserting the new bound unconditionally, which is false
% on this very model.
var 1..2: x :: output_var;
var 1..2: y :: output_var;
constraint int_ne(x, y);
solve satisfy;
